  <!-- 05 | CELL ARCHITECTURE -->

  <section class="portfolio-system">

    <div class="portfolio-section-header">

      <span>
        05 | Cell architecture
      </span>

      <h2>
        One robot. Multiple manufacturing functions.
      </h2>

    </div>


    <div class="system-grid">

      <figure class="system-overview">

        <img
          src="/images/projects/robotic-machining-cell/system-overview.jpg"
          alt="Overall SCANMILL 3D robotic machining cell architecture"
        />

        <figcaption>
          Cell architecture | Robot, workpiece, scan and machining systems
        </figcaption>

      </figure>


      <div class="subsystem-stack">

        <figure>

          <img
            src="/images/projects/robotic-machining-cell/end-effector.jpg"
            alt="Robotic machining end effector"
          />

          <figcaption>
            End effector | Spindle and finishing-tool integration
          </figcaption>

        </figure>


        <div class="subsystem-copy">

          <h3>
            Multi-function end effector
          </h3>

          <p>
            The robot must support different manufacturing functions while
            maintaining sufficient accessibility around the component.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spindle geometry, deburring equipment, tool length and robot wrist
            orientation therefore influence both mechanical integration and
            reachable machining configurations.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 06 | 3D SCANNING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      06
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        3D acquisition
      </h2>

      <p>
        The first stage of the workflow is the acquisition of the actual
        workpiece geometry.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 3D scan makes it possible to capture dimensional variations that are
        not represented by the nominal CAD model.
      </p>

      <p>
        This creates the foundation for a manufacturing process that reacts
        to the real component rather than to an idealised geometry.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 07 | RECONSTRUCTION -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      07
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Geometric reconstruction
      </h2>

      <p>
        Raw scanning data cannot directly be used as a machining definition.
      </p>

      <p>
        The acquired point cloud or mesh must therefore be cleaned, interpreted
        and converted into geometry suitable for trajectory generation.
      </p>

      <p>
        This reconstruction step creates the link between metrology and
        manufacturing.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- DIGITAL WORKFLOW -->

  <section class="portfolio-wide-visual">

    <figure>

      <img
        src="/images/projects/robotic-machining-cell/digital-workflow.jpg"
        alt="SCANMILL 3D digital manufacturing workflow"
      />

      <figcaption>
        Digital workflow | Scan → reconstruction → toolpath → robotic operation
      </figcaption>

    </figure>

  </section>


  <!-- 08 | ROBOTIC MILLING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      08
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Robotic milling
      </h2>

      <p>
        A machining spindle mounted on the robot provides a flexible alternative
        for operations where a conventional machine tool may not be necessary
        or where accessibility is particularly complex.
      </p>

      <p>
        The process must however account for the lower structural rigidity of
        an industrial robot compared with a dedicated CNC machine tool.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Cutting forces
        </li>

        <li>
          Robot configuration
        </li>

        <li>
          Tool orientation
        </li>

        <li>
          Structural compliance
        </li>

        <li>
          Vibration
        </li>

        <li>
          Path accuracy
        </li>
      </ul>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 09 | DEBURRING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      09
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Automated deburring
      </h2>

      <p>
        Deburring is particularly suited to robotic automation because the robot
        can orient the finishing tool around complex edges and three-dimensional
        component geometry.
      </p>

      <p>
        Integrating the deburring operation into the same digital workflow makes
        it possible to use reconstructed geometry to determine where material
        finishing is required.
      </p>

      <p>
        The objective is to reduce dependence on fixed trajectories when the
        geometry of the real component varies.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


  <!-- 10 | ADAPTIVE MANUFACTURING -->

  <section class="detail-section">

    <div class="detail-index">
      10
    </div>

    <div class="detail-copy">

      <h2>
        Adaptive manufacturing
      </h2>

      <p>
        The central idea behind SCANMILL 3D is to close the gap between
        measurement and manufacturing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Instead of following a fixed trajectory generated exclusively from
        nominal CAD geometry, the process can potentially adapt the operation
        according to the actual manufactured component.
      </p>

      <p>
        This creates a more flexible approach for parts with geometric variation,
        finishing requirements or complex free-form surfaces.
      </p>

    </div>

  </section>


